elow 100 °C — no bubbles, but still a bit of cloudiness. And if you’ve ever let a pot simmer gently (below full boil) you’ll see a little steam, proving that water can produce visible vapor without a rolling boil. ### The key: boiling forces rapid evaporation that makes condensation visible At 100 °C, the whole liquid reaches a temperature where vapor pressure equals atmospheric pressure. Vapor bubbles form inside the liquid and rise, dramatically increasing the surface area for evaporation. So much invisible vapor is produced that it rushes into the cooler air, immediately condenses into endless tiny droplets, and you get the thick white cloud you associate with “steam.” That’s the first time the effect is obvious, but not the first time water molecules left the pot. ### Connect it to the original sun question The sun evaporates water from puddles, lakes, and oceans exactly the same way: invisible vapor constantly escaping the surface. You never see “steam” rising from a warm puddle on a sunny day, yet it dries up. That’s proof that evaporation happens in the absence of any visible sign. So, your school science memory and your kitchen observation both point to boiling as the moment water “turns to steam” visibly. The missing piece is that visible steam is actually **re-condensed liquid**, and the true escape of water molecules (invisible evaporation) happens all along the way, but only becomes conspicuous when the rate is so high that the vapor can’t hide in the air anymore without clouding up.
