l truth — which doesn’t contradict your kitchen experience — is that water can also escape as invisible vapor at any temperature. You can verify this right now without boiling anything: - Place a small puddle of water on a plate and leave it on the counter for a day. You’ll never see a single wisp of steam, but the water will slowly disappear. That’s evaporation without boiling. - That’s exactly what the sun does to oceans and puddles. ### Why did this feel like a trick? Because visible steam is actually liquid. The white cloud you see at 100 °C is *not* water vapor (which is a transparent gas). It’s tiny liquid droplets that form when that invisible vapor hits cooler air and condenses back into a mist — just like your breath on a cold day. Before boiling, evaporating water molecules are still leaving, but they are few enough that they don’t condense into a visible cloud; they just mix invisibly into the air. So your eyes correctly report “no visible steam” until the rate becomes high enough to condense. That’s why you can trust your eyes *and* accept that evaporation happens quietly the whole time. So no, you haven’t been fooled, and your school didn’t set out to deceive you. They just taught you the most dramatic, visible example and used simplified language that stuck too well. You’re now seeing the bigger picture. The sun doesn’t boil water — it uses the invisible, surface-level escape route. And that, I promise, is not a trick but the everyday miracle behind drying puddles, wet laundry, and the entire water cycle.
